  "body": "Three gaps between what the web search stack fetches and what Claude actually receives. MINOR `4.18.3` → `4.19.0`.\n\n## 1. Extracted page content was discarded\n\nSeven adapters populate `WebSearchResultItem.content` (exa, tavily, firecrawl, jina, brave, ollama, parallel) and `_search_summary` already read it — but `_web_search_response_items` never copied it across, so the read was a branch that could never fire.\n\nThe effect: `EXA_CONTENTS`, `TAVILY_INCLUDE_RAW_CONTENT`, `FIRECRAWL_SCRAPE_FORMAT` are switchable in the admin UI, billed by the provider, captured by analytics, **rendered in the request detail modal** — and invisible to the model. The dashboard displayed page text Claude never got.\n\nContent now reaches the digest and is preferred over the snippet, under its own `WEBSEARCH_DIGEST_CONTENT_CHARS` cap (default 2000) so opting in isn't trimmed back to the 600-char snippet length.\n\n## 2. Domain filters never left the API layer\n\n`registry.search()`, every adapter, and the analytics input payload already accepted `allowed_domains`/`blocked_domains`. Nothing read them off the client's tool definition — leaving `SUPPORTS_DOMAINS` dead in production on five providers, and the recorded input payload hardcoded to `[]`.\n\nAnthropic declares these on the *tool*, not the tool call, so they arrive as extras on `Tool` (which permits them). They're now read and forwarded, and each attempt records the real values. `max_uses` is honoured as far as this single-shot path allows.\n\nA request carrying both lists resolves to the allow list rather than silently intersecting — Anthropic rejects that combination outright, so guessing an intersection would invent behaviour.\n\n## 3. Every failure reported `unavailable`\n\nAnthropic documents six `error_code` values and a client acts on the difference: `too_many_requests` is worth retrying later, an invalid input never is. We classify errors precisely and then discarded that at the final step.\n\n| Failure | Code |\n|---|---|\n| `WebSearchRateLimitError`, `WebSearchQuotaError` | `too_many_requests` |\n| `WebSearchInvalidRequestError` | `invalid_tool_input` |\n| exhausted `max_uses` budget | `max_uses_exceeded` |\n| anything else | `unavailable` |\n\nDeliberately no guessing at `query_too_long` or `request_too_large` — nothing classifies those confidently, so they stay `unavailable`.\n\n## Verification\n\nNew tests: content passthrough and digest preference; tool-option parsing including the both-lists case and absent parameters; end-to-end forwarding asserted on the `search_with_logging` call; the full error-code table. Existing digest-shape expectations updated for the additive `content` key, and five test doubles updated for the new keyword args.\n\n`ruff format`, `ruff check`, `ty check` clean; no suppressions.\n\nFull suite passes except **14 failures that also fail on unmodified `main`** — 8 from real API keys in the local environment defeating `Settings()` isolation, 6 PowerShell installer tests. Identical set before and after this change.\n\n## Residual risks\n\nContent preference increases input tokens per search for anyone who has content options enabled — that is the point, but it is a real cost change, tunable via `WEBSEARCH_DIGEST_CONTENT_CHARS=0` to disable.\n\n`user_location` is still not forwarded. It needs a country/locale translation layer across providers that spell it nine different ways (`BRAVE_COUNTRY`, `SERPER_GL`, `EXA_USER_LOCATION`, `FIRECRAWL_LOCATION`, …); that belongs in its own change rather than bolted on here.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
